00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.430
A question from Andy.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:08.640
This is one of my favorites.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.730
So there's a lot of ways to
run an application with Google

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:11.420
Cloud.

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:13.430
It's a huge platform.

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.290
How do I know what
I should choose?

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:16.340
Where do I run my stuff?

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.190
Should I run it on GKE,
Compute Engine, Cloud Run?

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:21.055
So many good choices.

00:07:21.055 --> 00:07:22.430
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, and this

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:24.080
is a question we get a lot.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.220
And it really just
depends on your situation.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:31.100
And the situations can be
a lot-- the type of team,

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:36.140
the size of team, and the
number of developers you have,

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:39.330
and the languages that
you might be using.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.630
So there are lots of
different scenarios

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:42.840
in which you can decide.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.060
So I'll give an
example of a few.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.000
So for example, Compute Engine.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.060
If you're migrating and you
just want to get from on-premise

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.970
infrastructure into cloud,
and you just want the speed--

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.190
you just want to get there--

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:58.820
I would choose Compute Engine
to just migrate as-is, and then

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.800
modernize later if needed.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.840
Sometimes, you don't
need to modernize

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:04.520
if you have licensing
requirements

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.180
and stuff like that.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.720
So that's Compute Engine.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.690
If you want to work with
containers-- need a little bit

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.510
more abstraction--
less abstraction,

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:17.210
you can work with
containers with GKE.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:18.980
And that gives you
a lot more control

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.690
over the number
of nodes you have,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.370
and the processing
that you're using.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.630
But if you might be just
wanting to run containers,

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:30.425
but don't want to manage the
underlying infrastructure,

00:08:30.425 --> 00:08:33.559
like nodes and stuff like
that, and the regions,

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:35.809
just use Cloud Run,
because it's serverless,

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:40.549
but it allows you to use
your container images

00:08:40.549 --> 00:08:42.650
and just deploy them.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.500
Cloud Functions is kind
of like everywhere.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:50.660
So you're trying to do--
handle one function, or a piece

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.840
or a feature of
code that you just

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.720
deploy in that
function as a Function

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.550
as a Code sort of service.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.530
But it kind of
applies everywhere.

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:01.850
So I wouldn't say--

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.460
so Cloud Function
is not like an OR--

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:05.690
it's more of an AND--

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.640
like it works with any of those.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:09.920
It's just more of an
extension and enhancement

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.030
of your services
with serverless.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.910
So I hope that helped
clarify a little bit of that,

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:17.360
but there's a lot that
goes in that decision.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:17.720
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah, I know.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.262
And I really liked
how you called out

00:09:19.262 --> 00:09:21.230
the Cloud Functions is
not an OR, it's an AND.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.835
It's-- Cloud Functions is
just fantastic at tying pieces

00:09:23.835 --> 00:09:24.335
together.

00:09:24.335 --> 00:09:26.002
It's one of the things
I love about it.

00:09:26.002 --> 00:09:27.710
And I'm just going to
point out for folks

