getting really good at
naming all those languages.

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:24.280
So from Selena-- ooh,
yes, great question.

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:27.583
When should I use GKE
Autopilot versus Cloud Run?

00:10:27.583 --> 00:10:29.500
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I'll
let you take that one.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:30.350
AJA HAMMERLY: You're going
to let me take this one?

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:31.030
OK.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:34.250
So this pretty
much boils down to,

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:36.910
do you want Kubernetes or not?

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:39.730
If you want Kubernetes, if you
want the enhanced flexibility

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.063
that Kubernetes has, or you
want to have all those knobs

00:10:42.063 --> 00:10:44.620
and dials that you can turn
to really fine tune everything

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:49.210
for your networking needs or
your particular load profile,

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:51.220
use GKE Autopilot.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:53.140
If you want to-- if
you have a container,

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.230
and you want to run it on
GCP, and that's your goal,

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:57.100
Cloud Run is great.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:58.708
Cloud Run is fantastic at that.

00:10:58.708 --> 00:11:00.250
And as we pointed
out, you don't even

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.590
need a container if you
use Cloud Run source

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:06.080
deploys for those
languages I just mentioned.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:07.560
More questions.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.320
Ooh, this one's
for you, Priyanka.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:10.970
This is from Caleb.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.060
What file formats are
supported with the Doc AI stuff

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:15.470
that Anya talked about?

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:16.637
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Oh, yeah.

00:11:16.637 --> 00:11:19.220
So you can do images and PDFs.

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.970
So it's really about
the unstructured image

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:25.610
data, so PDFs and images.

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.890
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:31.290
Let's see what else we got.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.420
Ooh, this one's from Mark.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:34.530
Another for you, Priyanka.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.270
Ooh, it's another
security question.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.750
So can you tell us more
about Binary Authorization?

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.588
We covered it very
briefly in the keynote,

00:11:41.588 --> 00:11:43.630
but it's something I've
been hearing a lot about,

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:44.940
and I'd love to know a
little more about it.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:45.940
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:48.810
So again, it kind of boils down
to the whole security narrative

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:50.640
that you mentioned--
that everybody

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.000
is kind of responsible
for the security

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.260
of the entire platform.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:12:01.200
So in this case, the binary auth
is really deploy time security.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:05.280
So you're deploying, and
making sure that your images--

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:09.960
or container images if you're
using GKE or Cloud Run--

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:11.130
works with binary auth.

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:15.420
So when you're at your
deployment stage, you have--

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:20.040
you can provide signature
authorizations on your images.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:27.210
So if-- and the verification
and the authorities for those.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:30.030
So if they are authorized,
only-- binary auth

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.620
will apply the authorizations.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:33.330
And once the image
is authorized,

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:35.743
only then you can deploy it.

00:12:35.743 --> 00:12:36.660
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:39.420
So I just got the signal
that we're running out

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:41.970
of time, so this is
going to, unfortunately,

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:43.890
be the last question.

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:46.080
And this question
comes from Wesley.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.360
Can I use the build
integrity features

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.690
with my on-prem software?

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:52.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hmm.

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.450
Yeah, so you kind of can.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:13:00.720
So with Cloud Build, it's
really any container image which

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.110
is built on Cloud Build,
you can use both on--

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:09.000
you can use it in both
on-prem or on Google Cloud.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:15.660
You just have to use the binary
auth attestation Cloud Build.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:19.720
And it's on the GitHub page,
so you can check that out.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:22.680
But if you're building
it in Cloud Build,

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:26.638
you can deploy it on-prem
or in Google Cloud.

00:13:26.638 --> 00:13:27.930
AJA HAMMERLY: Thanks, Priyanka.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.185
Well, that was a lot of fun.

00:13:29.185 --> 00:13:31.560
It was great to hear all the
questions from the audience.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.910
Y'all had some great ones.

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:34.710
And I want to say
just a huge thank you

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.752
to Priyanka for joining
us, and answering so many

00:13:36.752 --> 00:13:37.900
of those questions.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:40.927
Be sure to join us back
over on g.co/cloudnext,

00:13:40.927 --> 00:13:43.260
as the open infrastructure
spotlight will be kicking off

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.270
shortly, and they have
some amazing things that

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:46.380
they're going to show off.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.070
Thanks for joining us, everyone.

